<P align=center><FONT size=6><B><I>
DEC "Computer Lab"
</I></B></FONT></DIV><P>
<TABLE>
<TR>
<TD vAlign=top>
    <P><FONT size=3>
    <P>I have this cool DEC "Computer Lab".  It's a box with a power supply, 
some switches and lights, some logic, and a variable clock.  Here's a picture of 
the thing:
<TODO: Take a picture of the "Computer Lab">
<TABLE>
<TR>
  <TD>
    <A href="/pdp8/computerlab/clab.jpg">
    <IMG src="/pdp8/computerlab/clab.jpg" width=320>
    <BR>My Computer Lab, from eBay.
  </A></TD>
</TABLE>
    <P>You use jumper wires of various lengths to connect together the circuit 
you want to build.  There is a workbook (and a teacher's edition) of excercises 
that turn it into a course on computer logic.  Here are some pictures of the 
wires (I think these were taken by Ashley of his wires):
<TABLE>
<TR>
  <TD>
    <A href="/pdp8/computerlab/digitallab1.jpg">
    <IMG src="/pdp8/computerlab/digitallab1.jpg" width=320>
    <BR>The wires (color coded by length).
  </A></TD>
  <TD>
    <A href="/pdp8/computerlab/digitallab.jpg">
    <IMG src="/pdp8/computerlab/digitallab.jpg" width=320>
    <BR>A closeup of the funky tapered brass ends on the wires.
  </A></TD>
</TABLE>
    <P>There's an intermittent that is cured by flexing the box a 
little -- perhaps a bad solder joint or the like.  But other than 
that, it works great.
    <P>In case you were wondering, it does indeed use the same switches 
as the PDP-8/I :-).
</TD>
</TR>
</TABLE>
